      "dc:title": "LEAD AND COPPER OCCURRENCES IN BHAGALPUR DISTRICT, BIHAR.",
      "dc:creator": "Prasad U.",
      "prism:publicationName": "Indian Miner",
      "prism:volume": "29",
      "prism:issueIdentifier": "2",
      "prism:pageRange": "65-72",
      "prism:coverDate": "1975-01-01",
      "prism:coverDisplayDate": "Apr-Jun",
      "dc:description": "Lead and copper occurrences in the Bhagalpur district of Bihar, India, have been noted in nine areas. The mineralization has been observed in the pockets of tremolite-actinolite schist and hornblende-schist, and is indicated by the presence of malachite and azurite stains and occasional grains of galena. These mineralized pockets vary in size from 20 m. multiplied by 4. 5 m. to 300 m. multiplied by 25 m. Given the ever-increasing demands for base metals in India, the author feels that these small pockets should be exploited. Further, the exploitation of some of the smaller pockets, which would involve the opencast mining on a small scale, is considered to be economical, if done while prospecting these deposits.",
