      "dc:title": "Hydrological effects of urbanization: report of the sub group on the effects of urbanization on the hydrological environment, of the Coordinating Council of the International Hydrological Decade",
      "dc:creator": "McPherson M.B.",
      "prism:publicationName": "Unesco Press",
      "prism:pageRange": null,
      "prism:coverDate": "1974-01-01",
      "prism:coverDisplayDate": "1974",
      "dc:description": "Among the numerous studies undertaken by the International Hydrological Decade (IHD) was an investigation initiated in 1965 by the Working Group on the Influence of Man on the Hydrological Cycle and supported by the Food and Agricultural Organisation (FAO), charged with agricultural and urbanization aspects of the subject. The Coordinating Council in 1970 decided to form a Subgroup on the Effects of Urbanization on the Hydrological Environment, supported by Unesco, to assist the Working Group and to investigate more intensively industrial and urbanization aspects. In 1972 the IHD secretariat circulated to all national committees for the IHD a questionnaire on research and development needs. Responses are summarized in this report, and the respondents were among the participants at an International Workshop on the Hydrological Effects of Urbanization held in Warsaw, Poland, in November, 1973. A draft of Part I of this report, 'International Summary', was reviewed at the Workshop and the many invaluable observations and suggestions made by the participants were taken into account in the final publication. Part II of this report contains 'Case Studies' for Subgroup members' countries, and includes information collected in the Federal Republic of Germany, the Netherlands, Sweden, the United States of America and the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics. Part III is a group of 'Illustrative Special Topic Studies' prepared by individual members and associates from their countries, covering such subjects as urban runoff; effects of lignite mining on the urban water cycle; effects of industrial waste water and sludge on the self purification of rivers; some aspects of solid waste disposal; waste water dilution in rivers, lakes and reservoirs; synthetic detergents and water quality; effects of opencast mining on the water balance of an area; and the effects of urbanization on polder areas. One of the major findings of this report is that the field of urban hydrology is almost devoid of modern research investment and there has been relatively little study to date of the effect of urban man upon natural hydrological conditions, in spite of the significant economic and environmental importance of urban settlements in nearly every nation.",
